    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The affine and cyclotomic type-A Hecke algebras",
        "deps": [
          "def-cyclic-affine-type-a-cartan-datum",
          "def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra",
          "def-quantum-characteristic-of-an-invertible-hecke-parameter"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a field and $q\\in F^{\\times}$ with FINITE quantum characteristic $e=e(F,q)$, $2\\le e<\\infty$, residue set $I=\\mathbb Z/e\\mathbb Z$ with representatives $0,1,\\dots,e-1$ embedded in $F$, and the cyclic affine type-A Cartan datum with dominant weights $P^+$ and pairing $(\\Lambda,\\alpha_i)$ ([[def-quantum-characteristic-of-an-invertible-hecke-parameter]], [[def-cyclic-affine-type-a-cartan-datum]]). Fix $\\Lambda\\in P^+$ of level $\\ell=\\sum_{i\\in I}(\\Lambda,\\alpha_i)$ and put $\\lambda_i:=(\\Lambda,\\alpha_i)$.\n\n**(NG) Nondegenerate case $q\\ne1$.** The affine Hecke algebra of type $A_{d-1}$ at parameter $q$ is the unital $F$-algebra $H_d$ with generators $X_1^{\\pm1},\\dots,X_d^{\\pm1}$, $T_1,\\dots,T_{d-1}$ and relations (4.2)-(4.5) of the source: $X_rX_s=X_sX_r$, $X_rX_r^{-1}=1$, $T_rX_rT_r=qX_{r+1}$, $T_rX_s=X_sT_r$ ($s\\notin\\{r,r+1\\}$), $T_r^2=(q-1)T_r+q$, $T_rT_{r+1}T_r=T_{r+1}T_rT_{r+1}$, $T_rT_s=T_sT_r$ ($|r-s|>1$). The **cyclotomic Hecke algebra** is $H^\\Lambda_d:=H_d/\\langle\\prod_{i\\in I}(X_1-q^i)^{\\lambda_i}\\rangle$.\n\n**(DG) Degenerate case $q=1$** (then necessarily $\\operatorname{char}F=e>0$). The **degenerate affine Hecke algebra** $H_d$ is the unital $F$-algebra with generators $x_1,\\dots,x_d,s_1,\\dots,s_{d-1}$ and relations (3.2)-(3.5): $x_rx_s=x_sx_r$, $s_rx_{r+1}=x_rs_r+1$, $s_rx_s=x_ss_r$ ($s\\notin\\{r,r+1\\}$), $s_r^2=1$, braid and distant commutation; its ordered monomials $x_1^{m_1}\\cdots x_d^{m_d}w$ form an $F$-basis (the field-independent form of the presentation recorded in the source; the equivalent C-statement with its AC bookkeeping lives on the batch-17 page, which is outside this page's declared prerequisite closure and is deliberately not consumed here, see the batch notes). The **degenerate cyclotomic Hecke algebra** is $H^\\Lambda_d:=H_d/\\langle\\prod_{i\\in I}(x_1-i)^{\\lambda_i}\\rangle$.\n\n**Conventions.** $H^\\Lambda_0=F$; the cyclotomic polynomial is imposed on the FIRST polynomial generator; $T_r$ acts on positions $r,r+1$. The Hecke parameter is the one of [[def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]] ($T_i^2=(q-1)T_i+q$). It is NOT the parameter of [[def-the-affine-hecke-algebra-action-on-finite-tensor-powers-of-the-vector-representation]]: that page's generator satisfies $S^2=(Q-1)S+Q$ with $Q$ the square of its displayed parameter and $S=-q\\,t$, so in the present normalisation it carries the parameter $Q$, not $q$; the two normalisations must be translated by the dictionary recorded there and are not identified here. At level one $\\Lambda=\\Lambda_j$, $H^\\Lambda_d$ is the finite type-A Hecke algebra $H_{F,q}(S_d)$ if $q\\ne1$ and the group algebra $FS_d$ if $q=1$ ([[prop-cyclotomic-hecke-algebras-of-level-one-are-finite-hecke-algebras]]). Only finite $e$ is treated: the source's $e=0$ branches ($q$ not a root of unity, and the characteristic-zero degeneration, whose quiver is $A_\\infty$) are outside this page's cyclic scope and are deferred in the batch notes. No choice principle is used.",
        "strategy": "The two presentations are the source's (3.1)-(3.5) and (4.1)-(4.5); the cyclotomic quotient is its (1.2) with $(\\Lambda,\\alpha_i)=\\lambda_i$; the quotient exists as the quotient of a free associative algebra by a two-sided ideal, so the definition is well posed and $H^\\Lambda_0=F$. Reading order and the Hecke normalisation $T_r^2=(q-1)T_r+q$ match [[def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]], so the level-one identification of the proposition below applies to the library's finite Hecke algebra without rescaling. The parameter dictionary warning: [[def-the-affine-hecke-algebra-action-on-finite-tensor-powers-of-the-vector-representation]] uses a generator $S$ with $S^2=(Q-1)S+Q$ and $S=-q\\,t$ for its displayed parameters; read in the present normalisation its quadratic parameter is $Q$, the square of the present Hecke parameter, and the two normalisations must be translated by the explicit dictionary recorded on that page before any comparison. The finite-$e$ restriction: if $q\\ne1$ then $e$ is the multiplicative order of $q$, and if $q=1$ then $e=\\operatorname{char}F$, so the degenerate branch has positive characteristic; the $e=0$ (A-infinity) branch has no cyclic Cartan datum in the library and needs its own datum pair. No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
            {
              "title": "Jun Hu and Andrew Mathas, Seminormal forms and cyclotomic quiver Hecke algebras of type A, arXiv:1304.0906v3 (published in Math. Ann.), complete 51-page article",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1304.0906",
              "locator": "Introduction: Theorem A and Corollary 1.1, pp. 2-4; Section 2.2 Definition 2.2 and Remark 2.3 (cyclotomic Hecke algebras and degenerate specialization); Sections 2.5-2.6, Theorem 2.8, Definition 2.10, Remark 2.13, Theorem 2.14, Corollary 2.15, pp. 11-16; Section 4.1 Definition 4.1 (e-idempotent subrings)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Alexander Kleshchev, Representation theory of symmetric groups and related Hecke algebras, arXiv:0909.4844v1, Bull. AMS 47 (2010) 419-481 (complete 66-page survey)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4844",
              "locator": "Sections 2.7-2.8: Definition 2.7 and Theorem 2.8 with Corollaries 2.9-2.10 (pp. 12-13); Section 2.11 Definition 2.14 (affine KLR), pp. 22-23."
            },
            {
              "title": "Andrew Mathas, Hecke algebras and Schur algebras of the symmetric group, complete prepublication manuscript (AMS University Lecture Series 15, 1999), 79 pages",
              "url": "https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~bigelow/books/mathas.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapters 1-5, printed pp. 1-65: generic Hecke algebra, cellular and Murphy bases, Jucys-Murphy elements, decomposition matrices; the manuscript treats the finite type-A Hecke algebra (level one), not the affine/cyclotomic or KLR side."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the presentation is free and the quotient is by a two-sided ideal; no AC is used in this definition (the AC bookkeeping on the related batch-17 C-statement belongs to that page's realisation argument and is not consumed here).",
        "dependency_level": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The ordered-monomial basis and dimension of a cyclotomic Hecke algebra",
        "deps": [
          "def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras",
          "def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra",
          "thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra"
        ],
        "statement": "With the conventions of [[def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras]], the images of the ordered monomials $x_1^{m_1}\\cdots x_d^{m_d}w$ with $0\\le m_r<\\ell$, $w\\in S_d$ (degenerate case) respectively $X_1^{m_1}\\cdots X_d^{m_d}T_w$ with $0\\le m_r<\\ell$, $w\\in S_d$ (nondegenerate case) form an $F$-basis of $H^\\Lambda_d$. In particular $\\dim_F H^\\Lambda_d=\\ell^d d!$ and $H^\\Lambda_d$ is finite dimensional.",
        "strategy": "This is the source's basis theorem, quoted as [BK (3.6)] in the degenerate case and [BK (4.6)] in the nondegenerate case: both halves go back to [K1, Theorem 7.5.6] and [AK], and the full statement is restated as [Kleshchev survey, Theorem 2.11] (basis $\\{T_wX_1^{a_1}\\cdots X_d^{a_d}\\}$ with $0\\le a_r<\\ell$). The authoring step writes out the source's argument; its structural input in either case is that the affine Hecke algebra is free over its polynomial subalgebra with basis $\\{T_w\\}$, the cyclotomic relation being imposed in the FIRST variable only. Caution recorded for the author: for $r\\ge2$ the generator $X_r$ need NOT itself satisfy $\\prod_{i\\in I}(X_r-c_i)^{\\lambda_i}=0$ (degenerate level one with $d=2$ gives $x_1=0$ but $x_2=s_1\\ne0$ although $\\ell=1$), so the straightening/normal-form step must use the source's machinery, never a per-generator reduction of the cyclotomic polynomial. Both cases are proved separately in the source and use no choice principle.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
            {
              "title": "Andrew Mathas, Hecke algebras and Schur algebras of the symmetric group, complete prepublication manuscript (AMS University Lecture Series 15, 1999), 79 pages",
              "url": "https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~bigelow/books/mathas.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapters 1-5, printed pp. 1-65: generic Hecke algebra, cellular and Murphy bases, Jucys-Murphy elements, decomposition matrices; the manuscript treats the finite type-A Hecke algebra (level one), not the affine/cyclotomic or KLR side."
            },
            {
              "title": "Jun Hu and Andrew Mathas, Seminormal forms and cyclotomic quiver Hecke algebras of type A, arXiv:1304.0906v3 (published in Math. Ann.), complete 51-page article",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1304.0906",
              "locator": "Introduction: Theorem A and Corollary 1.1, pp. 2-4; Section 2.2 Definition 2.2 and Remark 2.3 (cyclotomic Hecke algebras and degenerate specialization); Sections 2.5-2.6, Theorem 2.8, Definition 2.10, Remark 2.13, Theorem 2.14, Corollary 2.15, pp. 11-16; Section 4.1 Definition 4.1 (e-idempotent subrings)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Alexander Kleshchev, Representation theory of symmetric groups and related Hecke algebras, arXiv:0909.4844v1, Bull. AMS 47 (2010) 419-481 (complete 66-page survey)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4844",
              "locator": "Theorem 2.11, p. 14 of the article: the basis $\\{T_wX_1^{a_1}\\cdots X_d^{a_d}\\}$ of the level-$\\ell$ cyclotomic Hecke algebra, attributed to [9] (Ariki-Koike) and [125, Theorem 7.5.6]."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the ordered-monomial basis theorem is quoted from the source, and the source's proof uses no choice principle.",
        "dependency_level": 2
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-cyclotomic-hecke-algebras-of-level-one-are-finite-hecke-algebras",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "title": "Level-one cyclotomic Hecke algebras are the finite Hecke algebras",
        "deps": [
          "def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras",
          "lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension",
          "def-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra",
          "thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $\\Lambda=\\Lambda_j$ be a level-one fundamental weight and keep the conventions of [[def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras]]. Then the assignments $T_r\\mapsto T_r$ ($1\\le r<d$) in the nondegenerate case and $s_r\\mapsto s_r$ in the degenerate case extend to $F$-algebra isomorphisms $H_{F,q}(S_d)\\to H^\\Lambda_d$ and $FS_d\\to H^\\Lambda_d$ respectively.",
        "strategy": "Surjectivity. The cyclotomic relation gives $X_1=q^j$ (resp. $x_1=j$), and the cross relation gives $X_{r+1}=q^{-1}T_rX_rT_r$ (resp. $x_{r+1}=s_rx_rs_r+s_r$); induction on $r$ shows every $X_r$ (resp. $x_r$) lies in the image of $F[T_1,\\dots,T_{d-1}]$ (resp. $F[s_1,\\dots,s_{d-1}]$), so the map is onto. Injectivity. [[lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension]] with $\\ell=1$ exhibits the $d!$ elements $T_w$ (resp. $w$) as a basis of $H^\\Lambda_d$, and [[thm-standard-basis-of-the-generic-type-a-hecke-algebra]] (resp. the group-algebra basis) gives the same dimension $d!$ for the source, so the surjection is an isomorphism. This is the source's level-one remark ([BK, p. 4]; [K, (2.34)]). No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
            {
              "title": "Alexander Kleshchev, Representation theory of symmetric groups and related Hecke algebras, arXiv:0909.4844v1, Bull. AMS 47 (2010) 419-481 (complete 66-page survey)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4844",
              "locator": "Sections 2.7-2.8: Definition 2.7 and Theorem 2.8 with Corollaries 2.9-2.10 (pp. 12-13); Section 2.11 Definition 2.14 (affine KLR), pp. 22-23."
            },
            {
              "title": "Andrew Mathas, Hecke algebras and Schur algebras of the symmetric group, complete prepublication manuscript (AMS University Lecture Series 15, 1999), 79 pages",
              "url": "https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~bigelow/books/mathas.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapters 1-5, printed pp. 1-65: generic Hecke algebra, cellular and Murphy bases, Jucys-Murphy elements, decomposition matrices; the manuscript treats the finite type-A Hecke algebra (level one), not the affine/cyclotomic or KLR side."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the recursion and the dimension count are explicit; no choice is used.",
        "dependency_level": 3
      },
      {
        "id": "def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Residue-sequence idempotents and cyclotomic Hecke blocks",
        "deps": [
          "def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras",
          "lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension",
          "def-blocks-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra-by-primitive-central-idempotents",
          "thm-hecke-jucys-murphy-action-is-dominance-triangular"
        ],
        "statement": "Keep the conventions of [[def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras]] and its finite dimensionality ([[lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension]]). For $1\\le r\\le d$ let $m_r(t)\\in F[t]$ be the minimal polynomial of $X_r$ (resp. $x_r$) acting on the regular module $H^\\Lambda_d$; its roots lie in the pairwise distinct set $\\{c_i: i\\in I\\}$ ($c_i=q^i$ resp. $i$) by the source's eigenvalue statement ([BK, §4.1], citing [G, Lemma 4.7]; [BK, §3.1], citing [K1, Lemma 7.1.2]). The Chinese-remainder decomposition of $F[t]/(m_r)$ gives canonical idempotents $\\varepsilon_r(i)$ in $F[X_r]\\subseteq F[X_r^{\\pm1}]$ (resp. in $F[x_r]$), the component at the factor $(t-c_i)^{e_{r,i}}$ with $e_{r,i}$ the multiplicity of $c_i$ in $m_r$ and $\\varepsilon_r(i):=0$ if $e_{r,i}=0$; for every finite-dimensional $H^\\Lambda_d$-module $M$ the element $\\varepsilon_r(i)$ acts as the projection onto the generalised eigenspace of $X_r$ at $c_i$. Put $e(i):=\\prod_{r=1}^d\\varepsilon_r(i_r)$ for $i=(i_1,\\dots,i_d)\\in I^d$, so that $e(i)M=M_i$ is the generalised simultaneous eigenspace of $X_1,\\dots,X_d$ (resp. $x_1,\\dots,x_d$) on every finite-dimensional $H^\\Lambda_d$-module $M$. Then:\n\n(i) the $e(i)$ are pairwise orthogonal idempotents lying in the commutative subalgebra generated by the polynomial generators, and $\\sum_{i\\in I^d}e(i)=1$;\n\n(ii) for $\\alpha\\in Q_+$ of height $d$ put $e_\\alpha:=\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}e(i)$; then $e_\\alpha$ is a central idempotent of $H^\\Lambda_d$, and $H^\\Lambda_d=\\bigoplus_{\\alpha}e_\\alpha H^\\Lambda_d$ is a direct sum of two-sided ideals; each nonzero algebra $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha:=e_\\alpha H^\\Lambda_d=e_\\alpha H^\\Lambda_d e_\\alpha$ is called a **block** of $H^\\Lambda_d$;\n\n(iii) (level-one dictionary) for $\\Lambda=\\Lambda_j$ the generalised eigenspaces of the polynomial generators are the generalised eigenspaces of the Hecke Jucys-Murphy elements; on a Murphy cell module the $k$-th generator acts dominance-triangularly with diagonal the q-contents of the node carrying $k$ ([[thm-hecke-jucys-murphy-action-is-dominance-triangular]]), so the nonzero $e(i)$ of a nonzero block occur precisely at the residue sequences read off standard tableaux;\n\n(iv) the source proves that each nonzero $e_\\alpha$ is a primitive central idempotent ([LM], [B, Theorem 1]); primitivity is recorded here but is NOT used anywhere on this page.",
        "strategy": "Well-definedness. (i) The regular module is finite dimensional by [[lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension]] and faithful, so the minimal polynomial $m_r$ exists and $m_r(X_r)=0$; its roots are among the pairwise distinct $c_i$'s by the source's eigenvalue lemma, so $F[t]/(m_r)$ is a product of local factors and the CRT gives orthogonal idempotents $\\varepsilon_r(i)\\in F[X_r]$ with $\\sum_i\\varepsilon_r(i)=1$. For a finite-dimensional module $M$ the minimal polynomial of $X_r$ on $M$ divides $m_r$ ($M$ is a quotient of a free module), so evaluating the CRT idempotents in $\\operatorname{End}(M)$ gives $e(i)M=M_i$, orthogonality and $\\sum_i e(i)=1$. The construction uses the source's eigenvalue statement for $X_r$; the cyclotomic polynomial itself is imposed in the FIRST generator only, and $X_r$ for $r\\ge2$ need not satisfy $\\prod_i(t-c_i)^{\\lambda_i}=0$. (ii) Centrality: for each finite-dimensional $H^\\Lambda_d$-module $M$ the summands $M_i$ satisfy $T_rM_i\\subseteq M_i+M_{s_r i}$ ([BK (4.7)], resp. $s_rM_i\\subseteq M_i+M_{s_r i}$ by (3.7)) and $X_rM_i\\subseteq M_i$; since $s_r i$ has the same $Q_+$-weight as $i$, the $\\alpha$-components $M^\\alpha=\\bigoplus_{i\\in I_\\alpha}M_i$ are submodules and the projecting idempotent $e_\\alpha$ acts as an $H^\\Lambda_d$-module endomorphism on every finite-dimensional module. By [[lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension]] the regular module is finite dimensional, and the left and right actions of $e_\\alpha$ on it coincide with the same endomorphism, so $e_\\alpha\\in Z(H^\\Lambda_d)$; alternatively the $S_d$-invariance of $I_\\alpha$ identifies $e_\\alpha$ with a symmetric polynomial in the generators. (iii) is the level-one residue dictionary of [[thm-hecke-jucys-murphy-action-is-dominance-triangular]] together with [[prop-cyclotomic-hecke-algebras-of-level-one-are-finite-hecke-algebras]]. (iv) is the source's cited theorem and is used nowhere below; the block decompositions used later only need (i)-(iii). No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
            {
              "title": "Alexander Kleshchev, Representation theory of symmetric groups and related Hecke algebras, arXiv:0909.4844v1, Bull. AMS 47 (2010) 419-481 (complete 66-page survey)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4844",
              "locator": "Sections 2.7-2.8: Definition 2.7 and Theorem 2.8 with Corollaries 2.9-2.10 (pp. 12-13); Section 2.11 Definition 2.14 (affine KLR), pp. 22-23."
            },
            {
              "title": "Andrew Mathas, Hecke algebras and Schur algebras of the symmetric group, complete prepublication manuscript (AMS University Lecture Series 15, 1999), 79 pages",
              "url": "https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~bigelow/books/mathas.pdf",
              "locator": "Chapters 1-5, printed pp. 1-65: generic Hecke algebra, cellular and Murphy bases, Jucys-Murphy elements, decomposition matrices; the manuscript treats the finite type-A Hecke algebra (level one), not the affine/cyclotomic or KLR side."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the spectral projections, the centrality computation and the block decomposition are explicit and choice-free.",
        "dependency_level": 5
      },
      {
        "id": "def-type-a-cyclotomic-specialization-of-the-qg-klr-algebras",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The type-A cyclotomic specialization of the KLR algebras",
        "deps": [
          "def-cyclic-affine-type-a-cartan-datum",
          "def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight",
          "def-quantum-characteristic-of-an-invertible-hecke-parameter",
          "def-quiver-hecke-klr-algebra-for-a-symmetrizable-cartan-datum"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F,q,e,I,\\Lambda,\\lambda_i$ be as in [[def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras]]. On the cyclic affine type-A Cartan datum define the type-A KLR datum $Q^{A}$ by $Q^{A}_{ij}=1$ if $a_{ij}=0$, $Q^{A}_{i,i+1}(u,v)=v-u$ and $Q^{A}_{i+1,i}(u,v)=u-v$ for the two edge orientations when $e\\ge3$, and $Q^{A}_{01}(u,v)=Q^{A}_{10}(u,v)=-(u-v)^2$ when $e=2$. Then $Q^{A}$ satisfies (Q1)-(Q4) of [[def-quiver-hecke-klr-algebra-for-a-symmetrizable-cartan-datum]]. Write $R_\\alpha$ for the resulting generalised KLR algebra over $F$ and $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ for the STANDARD cyclotomic quotient of [[def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight]] (monic first-strand relations $x_1^{\\lambda_{i_1}}e(\\nu)=0$). With $y_r:=x_r$ and $\\psi_r:=-\\tau_r$ the relations (1.6)-(1.15) of the source hold in $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$, and conversely the quotient presented by those relations is the same algebra; in other words $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ is the sign-modified cyclotomic Khovanov-Lauda algebra of the source for the cyclic quiver of quantum characteristic $e$.",
        "strategy": "(Q1)-(Q4): non-adjacent pairs have $a_{ij}=0$, $Q^{A}=1$ of degree $0$; an adjacent pair $(i,i+1)$, $e\\ge3$, has $b_{ij}=a_{ij}=-1$ and $Q^{A}_{i,i+1}=v-u$ is homogeneous of degree $2=-2b_{ij}$ with nonzero extremal coefficients of $u^{-a_{ij}}=u$ and $v^{-a_{ji}}=v$; the opposite orientation is the transpose and (Q2) is built into the two formulae; for $e=2$ the pair $0,1$ has $a_{01}=a_{10}=-2$, $b_{01}=-2$, and $-(u-v)^2$ is symmetric, homogeneous of degree $4=-2b_{01}$, with top coefficients $-1\\ne0$. Relation check with $\\psi=-\\tau$: (1.8) from $\\tau_re(i)=e(s_ri)\\tau_r$; (1.12)-(1.13) reduce to the dot-sliding constants $\\tau_ax_a-x_{a+1}\\tau_a=e(\\nu)$, $\\tau_ax_{a+1}-x_a\\tau_a=-e(\\nu)$ on equal colours, whose sign $\\psi=-\\tau$ converts into the source's constants $+1$; (1.14): equal colours $\\tau^2=Q_{ii}=0$; non-adjacent $\\tau^2=1=e(i)$; forward arrow $\\tau^2=v-u$ evaluated at $(y_r,y_{r+1})$ gives $y_{r+1}-y_r$; backward gives $y_r-y_{r+1}$; the $e=2$ double edge gives $(y_{r+1}-y_r)(y_r-y_{r+1})$; (1.15): the divided-difference numerator $[Q^{A}(x_a,x_{a+1})-Q^{A}(x_{a+2},x_{a+1})]/(x_{a+2}-x_a)$ equals $\\pm1$ in the two single-edge cases with $i_{r+2}=i_r$, equals $2y_{r+1}-y_r-y_{r+2}$ in the $e=2$ double-edge case and vanishes otherwise, and the global sign $\\psi=-\\tau$ matches the source's three cases. Degrees of products are read on the successive permuted colour pairs as in [[def-klr-grading-and-diagrammatic-composition]]. No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
