      {
        "id": "lem-cyclotomic-klr-block-satisfies-the-hecke-relations",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The cyclotomic KLR block satisfies the affine Hecke relations",
        "deps": [
          "def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight",
          "def-type-a-cyclotomic-specialization-of-the-qg-klr-algebras",
          "def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks",
          "lem-cyclotomic-klr-dot-annihilators-and-finite-root-strings",
          "lem-cyclotomic-hecke-intertwiners-and-local-power-series"
        ],
        "statement": "In the specialized cyclotomic KLR block $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ define $x_r:=\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}(y_r+i_r)e(i)$ and $s_r:=\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}(\\psi_rq_r(i)-p_r(i))e(i)$ (degenerate case), resp. $X_r:=\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}q^{i_r}(1-y_r)e(i)$ and $T_r:=\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}(\\psi_rQ_r(i)-P_r(i))e(i)$ (nondegenerate case), where $p,P,q,Q$ are the power series of [[lem-cyclotomic-hecke-intertwiners-and-local-power-series]] evaluated in $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ using the nilpotence of the $y$'s. Then these elements satisfy the defining relations (3.2)-(3.5), resp. (4.2)-(4.5), of the affine Hecke algebra and the cyclotomic relation $\\prod_i(x_1-i)^{\\lambda_i}=0$, resp. $\\prod_i(X_1-q^i)^{\\lambda_i}=0$; hence they define a unique $F$-algebra homomorphism $\\sigma:H^\\Lambda_d\\to R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$. Moreover $\\sigma$ kills $e_\\beta$ and every corner component $e_\\beta H^\\Lambda_de_\\gamma$ with $(\\beta,\\gamma)\\ne(\\alpha,\\alpha)$, and restricts to a unital homomorphism $\\sigma:H^\\Lambda_\\alpha\\to R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ with $\\sigma(e(i))=e(i)$ for $i\\in I_\\alpha$ and $\\sigma(e(i))=0$ otherwise.",
        "strategy": "This is [BK Theorem 3.3 with Lemma 3.4] (degenerate) and [BK Theorem 4.3 with Section 4.5] (nondegenerate). (a) The mixed, quadratic and braid relations among the displayed elements are the source's explicit five-case expansions, using (1.12)-(1.14), (3.43)/(4.44) and the defining properties (3.27)-(3.29)/(4.33)-(4.35) of the normalising power series; the cyclotomic relation vanishes because $y_1^{(\\Lambda,\\alpha_{i_1})}e(i)=0$ is (1.6). (b) $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ is finite dimensional by [[lem-cyclotomic-klr-dot-annihilators-and-finite-root-strings]], so the homomorphism makes it a finite-dimensional $H^\\Lambda_d$-module; the weight space of $\\sigma(x_r)=y_r+(\\text{colour})$ on it is exactly $e(i)R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ when $i\\in I_\\alpha$ and $0$ otherwise (nilpotence of the $y$'s and (1.7)); hence $\\sigma(e(i))$ is an idempotent projecting onto $e(i)R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$, so $\\sigma(e(i))=e(i)$ for $i\\in I_\\alpha$ and $\\sigma(e(i))=0$ otherwise. (c) Since $e_\\alpha$ is central and $\\sum_\\beta e_\\beta=1$ ([[def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks]]), $\\sigma(1-e_\\alpha)=0$ and $\\sigma$ annihilates all components with $(\\beta,\\gamma)\\ne(\\alpha,\\alpha)$; it therefore factors through $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ and is unital there. No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Graded decomposition numbers for cyclotomic Hecke algebras, arXiv:0901.4450v3, Adv. Math. 222 (2009) 1883-1942 (complete 57-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0901.4450",
              "locator": "Section 2.2 relations (2.9)-(2.12) and the grading statement after (2.12); Sections 4.1-4.3, Theorem 4.1 and Remark 4.2, pp. 30-32."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the relation checks and the finite-dimensional weight-space computation are explicit; no choice is used.",
        "dependency_level": 7
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "The Brundan-Kleshchev blockwise Hecke-KLR isomorphism",
        "deps": [
          "def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks",
          "lem-hecke-intertwiners-produce-local-klr-generators",
          "lem-cyclotomic-klr-block-satisfies-the-hecke-relations"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F$ be an algebraically closed field, $q\\in F^{\\times}$ with finite quantum characteristic $e=e(F,q)\\ge2$, $\\Lambda\\in P^+$ dominant, and $\\alpha\\in Q_+$ of height $d$ with $e_\\alpha\\ne0$ (the conventions of [[def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras]] and [[def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks]]). Then the homomorphisms $\\rho:R^\\Lambda_\\alpha\\to H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ of [[lem-hecke-intertwiners-produce-local-klr-generators]] and $\\sigma:H^\\Lambda_\\alpha\\to R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ of [[lem-cyclotomic-klr-block-satisfies-the-hecke-relations]] are mutually inverse $F$-algebra isomorphisms. Equivalently, $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ is generated by the Khovanov-Lauda generators $\\{e(i)\\}\\cup\\{y_r\\}\\cup\\{\\psi_r\\}$ subject only to the relations (1.6)-(1.15), and the isomorphism is normalised by $\\rho(e(i))=e(i)$ and $\\rho(y_re(i))=(1-q^{-i_r}X_r)e(i)$ if $q\\ne1$, resp. $\\rho(y_re(i))=(x_r-i_r)e(i)$ if $q=1$. The degenerate ($q=1$, $\\operatorname{char}F=e$) and nondegenerate ($q\\ne1$) cases are proved separately and both hold in finite quantum characteristic $e$.",
        "strategy": "This is the Main Theorem of [BK]; the proof is completed in [BK Section 3.5] (degenerate) and [BK Section 4.5] (nondegenerate). The composite $\\sigma\\circ\\rho$ is the identity on the generators $e(i),y_r,\\psi_r$ of $R^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ and $\\rho\\circ\\sigma$ is the identity on the generators of $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$: for the polynomial generators this is $\\rho(\\sigma(x_r))=\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}x_re(i)=x_r$ (using $\\sum_{i\\in I_\\alpha}e(i)=e_\\alpha$), and the remaining checks are the source's displayed computations (3.50)-(3.51) and their nondegenerate analogues. The normalisation clause is [BK4 Theorem 4.1] of the second reference with $\\xi=q$. Both maps exist by the two preceding lemmas, so no further input is used; the source's dimension formula (3.6)/(4.6) is not needed for the isomorphism. No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Graded decomposition numbers for cyclotomic Hecke algebras, arXiv:0901.4450v3, Adv. Math. 222 (2009) 1883-1942 (complete 57-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0901.4450",
              "locator": "Section 2.2 relations (2.9)-(2.12) and the grading statement after (2.12); Sections 4.1-4.3, Theorem 4.1 and Remark 4.2, pp. 30-32."
            },
            {
              "title": "Jun Hu and Andrew Mathas, Seminormal forms and cyclotomic quiver Hecke algebras of type A, arXiv:1304.0906v3 (published in Math. Ann.), complete 51-page article",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1304.0906",
              "locator": "Introduction: Theorem A and Corollary 1.1, pp. 2-4; Section 2.2 Definition 2.2 and Remark 2.3 (cyclotomic Hecke algebras and degenerate specialization); Sections 2.5-2.6, Theorem 2.8, Definition 2.10, Remark 2.13, Theorem 2.14, Corollary 2.15, pp. 11-16; Section 4.1 Definition 4.1 (e-idempotent subrings)."
            },
            {
              "title": "Alexander Kleshchev, Representation theory of symmetric groups and related Hecke algebras, arXiv:0909.4844v1, Bull. AMS 47 (2010) 419-481 (complete 66-page survey)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.4844",
              "locator": "Sections 2.7-2.8: Definition 2.7 and Theorem 2.8 with Corollaries 2.9-2.10 (pp. 12-13); Section 2.11 Definition 2.14 (affine KLR), pp. 22-23."
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "None: the isomorphism is exhibited by explicit mutually inverse maps; no choice is used.",
        "dependency_level": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "cor-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks-inherit-a-nontrivial-z-grading",
        "kind": "corollary",
        "title": "Cyclotomic Hecke blocks inherit a nontrivial Z-grading",
        "deps": [
          "thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism",
          "def-klr-grading-and-diagrammatic-composition",
          "def-cyclotomic-klr-quotient-at-a-dominant-weight"
        ],
        "statement": "In the situation of [[thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism]], transporting the grading of [[def-klr-grading-and-diagrammatic-composition]] across the isomorphism gives a $\\mathbb Z$-grading on the block $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$; it is the unique grading in which $e(i)$ has degree $0$, $y_r$ has degree $2$ and $\\psi_re(i)$ has degree $-a_{i_r,i_{r+1}}$, and it is independent of the chosen normalising power series up to graded isomorphism. This grading is not visible on the ungraded presentation of $H^\\Lambda_d$: the Hecke relations $T_r^2=(q-1)T_r+q$ (resp. $s_r^2=1$) are not homogeneous for any assignment of degrees to the Hecke generators, and the elements $s_r,T_r$ themselves are sums of homogeneous pieces of different degrees.",
        "strategy": "Transport the homogeneous decomposition of [[def-klr-grading-and-diagrammatic-composition]] through the algebra isomorphism of [[thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism]]; purity of the transport follows because $\\rho$ preserves degrees on the generators used to define the grading. Uniqueness holds because the graded generators generate $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$, and independence of the auxiliary power series follows since different admissible choices differ by units of degree $0$ (the difference in the source's (3.27)-(3.29)/(4.33)-(4.35) is measured in the degree-$0$ units). The failure of homogeneity on the Hecke generators is immediate from the mixed degrees in $s_r=\\sum_i(\\psi_rq_r(i)-p_r(i))e(i)$, whose constant term does not vanish in the unequal-colour case. This is [BK Corollary 1]. No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jonathan Brundan and Alexander Kleshchev, Blocks of cyclotomic Hecke algebras and Khovanov-Lauda algebras, arXiv:0808.2032v3, Invent. Math. 178 (2009) 451-484 (complete 32-page article)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.2032",
              "locator": "Introduction pp. 1-5 (quiver, (1.1)-(1.4), Main Theorem (1.6)-(1.15), Corollaries 1-2); Section 2 (2.1)-(2.7), Lemmas 2.1-2.2; Sections 3.1-3.5 pp. 7-19; Sections 4.1-4.5 pp. 19-27; Section 6 pp. 29-31."
            },
