      {
        "id": "cex-the-whole-affine-hecke-algebra-is-not-one-cyclotomic-klr-block",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "title": "The whole affine Hecke algebra is not one cyclotomic KLR block",
        "deps": [
          "def-affine-and-cyclotomic-type-a-hecke-algebras",
          "lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension",
          "def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks",
          "thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism"
        ],
        "statement": "Neither side of the Brundan-Kleshchev isomorphism may be replaced by the whole algebra: (i) the affine Hecke algebra $H_1$ is $F[x_1^{\\pm1}]$ resp. $F[x_1]$, hence infinite dimensional, while every cyclotomic block $H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ is finite dimensional of dimension $\\le\\ell^dd!$; (ii) even after imposing the cyclotomic relation, a single residue-content idempotent $e_\\alpha$ cuts out a proper factor: at level one with $d=2$, $e=3$ and $q$ a primitive cube root, $H^\\Lambda_2\\cong H_{F,q}(S_2)\\cong F\\times F$ has two nonzero blocks $H^\\Lambda_{\\alpha_0+\\alpha_1}$ and $H^\\Lambda_{\\alpha_0+\\alpha_2}$, so $H^\\Lambda_2$ is not isomorphic to either one of them, and on the KLR side $R^\\Lambda_2=R^\\Lambda_{\\alpha_0+\\alpha_1}\\oplus R^\\Lambda_{\\alpha_0+\\alpha_2}$ is not isomorphic to a single block.",
        "strategy": "For (i): by definition $H_1$ has the single invertible generator $x_1$, so $H_1=F[x_1^{\\pm1}]$ is infinite dimensional (its powers are linearly independent) in the nondegenerate case and $F[x_1]$ in the degenerate case; the bound on $\\dim H^\\Lambda_\\alpha$ is [[lem-cyclotomic-hecke-algebra-basis-and-dimension]] together with the block decomposition [[def-residue-sequence-idempotents-and-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks]]. For (ii): the two blocks and their dimensions are the computation of [[ex-level-one-rank-two-cyclotomic-klr-algebra-at-e-three]], and the theorem [[thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism]] applies to each nonzero block separately; a direct sum of two nonzero algebras is never isomorphic to one of its summands. No choice is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
